<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/William_Carey.jpg/960px-William_Carey.jpg" alt="Portrait of William Carey" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" loading="lazy" width="960" height="1439" />

<p>In 1785, a twenty-four-year-old shoemaker in the village of Hackleton, Northamptonshire, kept a bonnet-maker's shop and a cobbler's bench and preached on alternate Sundays to a small Baptist congregation. He had left school at twelve. He had never set foot in a university, had never been taught by anyone with a degree, and would not for another eight years.</p>

<p>That young man, William Carey, would go on to write grammars and dictionaries for languages that had never been printed at all, translate the Bible into six Indian languages, and supervise a press that put Scripture into 44 languages and dialects in his own lifetime. He learned Sanskrit in his forties, in a country he reached for the first time at thirty-one, speaking not one word of any language spoken there.</p>

<p>He was not a natural polyglot in the way the popular imagination means it. Asked how he should be described after his death, he gave an answer that is worth pinning to the wall of any learner who has ever felt too late, too poor or too badly schooled to begin:</p>

<blockquote>Eustace, if after my removal any one should think it worth his while to write my Life, I will give you a criterion by which you may judge of its correctness. If he give me credit for being a plodder he will describe me justly. Anything beyond this will be too much. I can plod. I can persevere in any definite pursuit. To this I owe everything. &mdash; <strong>William Carey, quoted in George Smith, The Life of William Carey, Shoemaker and Missionary (1885)</strong></blockquote>

<h2>A Cobbler's Son Who Wanted the Whole World</h2>

<p>Carey was born on 17 August 1761 in the hamlet of Pury End, in the parish of Paulerspury in Northamptonshire, the eldest of five children. His parents, Edmund and Elizabeth Carey, were weavers. When William was six, his father was appointed parish clerk and village schoolmaster, which gave the boy something valuable and unremarkable: access to books, and a father who could read them with him.</p>

<p>What he had, by every account, was appetite. He collected plants and animals, kept a small private menagerie, and was drawn to botany in a way that would resurface four decades later when he founded the Agri-Horticultural Society of India. He also had, as the record puts it, a natural gift for language, and taught himself Latin. There was no schoolmaster in the story at that point. There was a boy and a grammar.</p>

<p>At fourteen, his father apprenticed him to a cordwainer in the nearby village of Piddington, a shoemaker named Clarke Nichols. Carey's formal education effectively ended there. The apprenticeship, however, turned out to contain an entire education, because of two things that happened in the shop: he met a fellow apprentice named John Warr, a Dissenter, and he met a local weaver named Thomas Jones.</p>

<h2>Latin at the Bench, Greek with a Weaver's Help</h2>

<p>The first of those two men changed what Carey believed. The second changed what he could read.</p>

<p>Thomas Jones was a weaver in the neighbourhood who had received a classical education &mdash; the kind of man that the English provinces quietly produced, and the kind that a formal history of linguistics usually ignores. He helped Carey with Greek. Between Jones's guidance, a borrowed Latin grammar and his own hours at the bench, Carey accumulated the classical languages that would otherwise have been the property of men who had attended grammar school until sixteen.</p>

<p>His professional life was not going well in material terms. In 1779 Nichols died, and Carey went to work for the shoemaker Thomas Old, whose sister-in-law Dorothy Plackett he married in 1781 in the church at Piddington. The marriage register contains Dorothy's mark &mdash; a crude cross &mdash; because she could not write her name. In a workshop where the master's sister-in-law signed with a cross, Carey was reading Latin and Greek.</p>

<p>Old died soon afterwards and Carey took over the business. It was then that his method became fully visible in the historical record, and it is the detail to keep: he taught himself Greek, Italian, Dutch and French, <strong>often reading while working on the shoes</strong>.</p>

<h2>Six Languages Learned Around a Shoemaker's Stool</h2>

<p>This is the part that repays attention, because it dismantles the standard excuse. Carey did not have a study. He had a stool, a lap, an awl, and customers waiting. What he had that most learners claim to lack was not time but a rule: no day without the work.</p>

<p>The scale of what he accumulated that way is easy to underread. By the time he was in his mid-twenties, he had Latin and Greek from a dead man's books and a weaver's patience, and Italian, Dutch and French from hours stolen between boots. He was also, since 5 October 1783, a Baptist, baptised by John Ryland, and in 1785 he was appointed schoolmaster at Moulton while also pastoring the local Baptist church.</p>

<p>On 10 August 1786 he was ordained as a Baptist minister &mdash; and continued working as a shoemaker. He referred to himself as a cobbler for the rest of his life, and one of his biographers gave the book the title <em>William Carey the Shoemaker Who Became the Father and Founder of Modern Missions</em>. In 1789 he became full-time pastor of the Harvey Lane Baptist Church in Leicester.</p>

<p>Reading is what changed the shape of his ambition. He read Jonathan Edwards's account of the life of David Brainerd, and the journals of Captain James Cook, and the two together did something specific: they filled his head with the rest of the world and with the arithmetic of how little had been done about it.</p>

<h2>The Sermon That Started a Movement</h2>

<p>In 1792 Carey published a short book whose title is a fair summary of its argument: <em>An Enquiry into the Obligations of Christians to Use Means for the Conversion of the Heathens</em>. It had five parts. The third is the one that reveals what kind of mind he had: twenty-six pages of tables listing the area, population and religion of every country in the world &mdash; statistics he had compiled himself, over years, as a village schoolteacher.</p>

<p>Then, preaching from Isaiah 54:2&ndash;3 in what became known as the Deathless Sermon, he used the sentence that has followed his name ever since:</p>

<blockquote>Expect great things from God; attempt great things for God. &mdash; <strong>William Carey, the Deathless Sermon, 1792</strong></blockquote>

<p>In October 1792 the Particular Baptist Society for the Propagation of the Gospel Amongst the Heathen was founded, the body now known as BMS World Mission. Carey was a charter member alongside Andrew Fuller, John Ryland and John Sutcliff. The Society needed somewhere to send its first missionary, and a doctor named John Thomas, recently back from Calcutta and raising funds, supplied the answer.</p>

<h2>"Difficulty of Learning the Language" Was One of the Objections He Answered</h2>

<p>One of the five parts of the <em>Enquiry</em> exists specifically to answer objections to the whole enterprise, and it names two that Carey knew intimately: difficulty learning the language, and danger to life.</p>

<p>He wrote about the language problem as a man who had learned five European languages at a bench and expected to learn more. He was wrong about the size of the task in one respect and right about it in the way that mattered: it could be done by plodding, and there was no shortcut.</p>

<p>He sailed in April 1793 with his eldest son Felix, and with Thomas and his family, and with his wife Dorothy &mdash; who had refused to go until, pregnant with their fourth son, she agreed on condition that her sister came too. The ship's captain was warned that carrying them would endanger his command, because their unauthorised passage violated the East India Company's trade monopoly, and he sailed without them. They waited until June, found a Danish captain, and landed at Calcutta in November 1793.</p>

<p>Carey was thirty-two, with five European languages, no Asian language, no salary, no permission to be in the country, and a mission whose survival depended on becoming intelligible to people who had never heard of him.</p>

<h2>Bengali Learned from Scratch, Between Indigo Accounts</h2>

<p>Within the first year the missionaries set about learning Bengali, because there was no other way to speak to anyone. To support themselves, Carey moved his family west to Midnapore, where a friend of Thomas owned two indigo factories and needed managers.</p>

<p>He did that job for six years. It is the least glamorous chapter of his life and arguably the most instructive: a man in his thirties running dye works and indigo accounts, in a language he was still learning, in a climate that killed his five-year-old son Peter in 1800. During those years he completed the first revision of his Bengali New Testament, and worked out the principles the mission would run on: communal living, financial self-reliance, and the training of local ministers rather than imported ones.</p>

<p>At the same time, in 1800, the East India Company's hostility pushed the mission into the Danish colony of Serampore, where Carey joined the printer William Ward and the schoolteacher Joshua Marshman. The three became known as the Serampore trio. Ward set up a print shop with a secondhand press Carey had acquired, and began the job of printing the Bible in Bengali &mdash; the first Bengali New Testament came off that press in 1801.</p>

<h2>The Munshi, the Pundits, and Sanskrit in His Forties</h2>

<p>In 1801 the Governor-General founded Fort William College to train civil servants, and offered Carey the professorship of Bengali. It was an extraordinary appointment for a Northamptonshire cobbler, and it gave him something no cobbler can buy: colleagues who were native scholars, and the time to be corrected.</p>

<p>His colleagues included pundits whom he consulted to correct his Bengali testament, and a <em>munshi</em> named Ram Ram Basu. It was from Ram Ram Basu that Carey learned Sanskrit and other Indian languages. He was past forty. He did not learn Sanskrit from a book in a shop; he learned it from a living speaker who was paid to teach him and who corrected him daily. That is worth stating plainly, because Carey's story is often told as pure autodidacticism, and it was not. He combined two things that most self-taught learners neglect: relentless private work, and hired, expert, human correction the moment he could afford it.</p>

<p>The correction was reciprocated in kind. At the college he produced <em>A Grammar of the Bengalee Language</em> in 1801, wrote grammars of Bengali and Sanskrit, and in 1805 published the first book on Marathi grammar. He served as professor of Bengali, Sanskrit and Marathi for thirty years.</p>

<h2>A Press for Languages That Had Never Been Printed</h2>

<p>The Serampore press is where Carey's languages became permanent. It is credited as the only press that "consistently thought it important enough that costly fonts of type be cast for the irregular and neglected languages of the Indian people" &mdash; and the type largely meant Ward cutting punches by hand, character by character.</p>

<p>Prior to Carey's stint, printing in these languages had barely happened at all. His team produced textbooks, dictionaries and classical literature for primary-school children, college students and the general public, including the first systematic Sanskrit grammar, which served as a model for later work. He translated the Hindu epic the Ramayana into English. He translated the Bible into Bengali, Oriya, Marathi, Hindi, Assamese and Sanskrit, with portions in many other languages and dialects.</p>

<p>In his lifetime, the mission printed and distributed the Bible in whole or in part in <strong>44 languages and dialects</strong>. He also used his influence with the Governor-General against infanticide and against sati, after consulting the pundits and establishing that neither practice had a basis in the Hindu sacred writings. Sati was formally abolished in 1829.</p>

<h2>The Fire of 1812 and What Burned With It</h2>

<p>On 11 March 1812, fire destroyed the print shop at Serampore. The damage was put at £10,000, which was catastrophic for a self-funded mission, but the real loss was different in kind. The irreplaceable manuscripts went up with the press: a great part of Carey's translation of Sanskrit literature, and a polyglot dictionary of Sanskrit and related languages that would have stood as a major work of philology had it survived.</p>

<p>The press itself and Ward's punches were saved, and the mission was printing again within six months. Decades of a man's work had vanished, along with the one book only he was in a position to write. He started again, and kept working. Any learner who has had a hard drive fail, or lost a year of notes, or watched a project collapse and had to decide whether to rebuild, might find that the most useful paragraph in this biography.</p>

<h2>Serampore College and the First Degree-Granting Institution in Asia</h2>

<p>In 1818 the mission founded Serampore College to train ministers and to teach arts and sciences to anyone, regardless of caste or country. In 1827 King Frederick VI of Denmark granted a royal charter making it a degree-awarding institution &mdash; the first in Asia.</p>

<p>Carey also founded the Agri-Horticultural Society of India at Alipore in 1820, and when William Roxburgh went on leave, was entrusted with the Botanical Garden at Calcutta. The genus <em>Careya</em> is named after him, which is a fitting monument for a boy who had grown plants in his bedroom in Pury End.</p>

<p>His private life was heavy. Dorothy's mental breakdown, which began after the death of their son, never lifted; she died in 1807. He married Charlotte Rhumohr in 1808, a Danish member of his church whom the record describes as his intellectual equal; she died in 1821, as did his eldest son Felix. In 1823 he married a widow named Grace Hughes. Late in life, disputes within the Society he had founded became irreconcilable, and he severed ties with it, moving onto the college grounds. He died at Serampore on 9 June 1834, revising his Bengali Bible. The couch he died on is kept at Regent's Park College, Oxford.</p>

<h2>The Other Side of the Ledger</h2>

<p>Honest history owes this story a second section, because Carey's work was not politically neutral and he was not a disinterested philologist.</p>

<p>Historians including the Comaroffs, Thorne, Van der Veer and Brian Pennington have argued that the missionary reports from Serampore &mdash; which are the main surviving source for much of what is known about Carey &mdash; have to be read within their evangelical and colonial ideology. The Indian historian V. Rao has documented Carey's lack of tolerance and respect for Indian culture, noting that he described Indian music as "disgusting" and dismissed practices as "dishonorable" to God, and that these attitudes shaped the literature he and his colleagues produced.</p>

<p>Carey himself recommended that his fellow Anglo-Indians learn and interpret Sanskrit in a manner "compatible with colonial aims", writing that knowledge of Sanskrit was valuable because "to gain the ear of those who are thus deceived, it is necessary for them to believe that the speaker has a superior knowledge of the subject." The man who cast type for neglected Indian languages also calculated that mastery of them was leverage over the people who spoke them.</p>

<p>Both things are true and both belong in the account. What the learner can take from this is narrower but real: the same technique can serve purposes you would endorse and purposes you would not, and the history of language learning is inseparable from the history of power. It is worth knowing whose voices translated the dictionary, and why.</p>

<h2>What Carey's Method Actually Was</h2>

<p>Strip away the mission and what remains is one of the best-documented self-directed language-learning methods in history, and it is startlingly ordinary.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>He worked in the gaps.</strong> Greek, Italian, Dutch and French were learned while mending shoes, not in protected study hours. Reading happened during work, not instead of it.</li>
  <li><strong>He borrowed the first grammar and asked a real person for the rest.</strong> Thomas Jones, a weaver, taught him Greek; Ram Ram Basu, a munshi, taught him Sanskrit. He never confused solitary work with teaching himself everything.</li>
  <li><strong>He paid for correction as soon as he could.</strong> At Fort William College he had pundits check his translations. A native speaker who corrects you is worth more than another hundred pages.</li>
  <li><strong>He built artefacts, not just knowledge.</strong> A grammar. A dictionary. A translation. A textbook for schoolchildren. Production is what shows you where your understanding is hollow.</li>
  <li><strong>He stayed in the language every day for forty years.</strong> His Bengali Bible was still being revised on the couch he died on.</li>
  <li><strong>He measured scale honestly.</strong> Twenty-six pages of population tables before he tried to persuade anyone of anything. He was a man who counted.</li>
  <li><strong>He outlived catastrophic loss once, and started again.</strong> The 1812 fire took irreplaceable work; he resumed printing within six months.</li>
  <li><strong>He described himself accurately.</strong> Not brilliant, not gifted. A plodder. "To this I owe everything."</li>
</ul>

<p>The last point is the one to keep. Carey's own account of his success is not that he was unusually able, but that he was unusually persistent in a single defined pursuit. That is a description of a habit, not a talent, and habits can be acquired by anyone at any age. He learned Sanskrit after forty. He learned Bengali from zero at thirty-two. He published his first book on Marathi grammar at forty-four.</p>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>

<p>Everything Carey built came out of a cobbler's shop, a borrowed Latin grammar and a refusal to stop. There is no part of that story that requires genius, boarding school, money or youth &mdash; only the two things he named himself: a definite pursuit, and the willingness to plod at it.</p>

<p>That is also the honest argument for a flashcard habit. A deck kept alive for a year is not glamorous, and it does not feel like the story of a man who gave forty-four languages their first printed type. But it is the same mechanism. Learn the grammar you can get, get corrected by someone real, produce something, and come back tomorrow. Thirty years of that at a bench in Northamptonshire turned a shoemaker into a professor of Sanskrit in Calcutta. Your version can be smaller. It still has to be daily.</p>

